---

I've been wanting a dedicated "dev machine" for a few months now, rather than just using
my desktop (yes I realize this is a very privileged desire, my desktop works fine and is a
great compact little machine that I mostly use for gaming and general web surfing
(probably too much of the latter)). However, since computers now cost approximately [one
million dollars](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=l91ISfcuzDw) (lick my balls, Altman et al), I've been looking around
for a cheap beater laptop that I could bring back to life (read: install Linux on). Lucky
for me, a family member had an old 13" MacBook Air from 2020 (the one with the intel chip)
that I was able to pick up when visiting the US last month. The battery was completely
dead, so I picked up a [replacement kit from Ifixit](https://www.ifixit.com/en-eu/products/macbook-pro-13-retina-mid-2019-battery) for 100 euros,
bringing the total cost for a perfectly functioning brand new laptop to a whopping... 100
euros (and it could have been less if I just got the battery, but I wanted the tools for
the future). Here is my stream of consciousness of getting it to a working state:
* battery shot to all hell
* OS also shot to all hell, can only boot into recovery mode
* had to go to a cafe to do internet recovery because it no like my home internet (stupid
fucking local VLAN I have no control over)
* success!
* updated all macos shit (but can't go past Sequoia because liquid glass is too cool for
me (but I'm happy about it because liquid glass is ugly as shit))
* replaced battery:
[Spent half an hour slowly dissolving gule and prying the old battery out]($image.asset('old-battery-out.jpg'))
[Tiniest screw I've ever seen, whoever designed this fuck you (even though I'm sure it was for good reason)]($image.asset('fuck-this-screw.jpg'))
[Slap a new bad boy in there]($image.asset('new-battery-in.jpg'))
* tried to install t2arch, did not likey (probably user error tho)
* installed fedora instead
* success!
* until I rebooted after updating stuff (probably did too fast and made mistake of going
via software center rather than `dnf` like I should have)
* Now can't get to login screen, just black screen. cool.
* rage!
* But at least it boots into grub, so ok let me drop into a shell and unfuck it
* Simply edit kernel params in grub, ezpz
* ctrl-x doesn't boot, it just puts an 'x' character.
* Fuck.
* simply use f10 instead!
* There's no f10 key on macbook because it's the touchbar one
* Fuck.
* rage!
* try external keyboard #1, via a usbc adapter. Fail.
* try my main desktop keyboard. I don't have Fn keys configured on a layer and don't want
to reflash my whole ass keyboard. Fail.
* rage!
* start considering reinstalling fedora or trying arch again
* wait I have my old ergodox ez
* pull that off the shelf
* edit kernel params and hit the f10 key on layer 3 of ergodox:
[Fear my awesome problem solving power]($image.asset('ergo-to-rescue.jpg'))
* success!
* boot into shell at the tiniest fucking resolution of all time
* `sudo dnf upgrade`
* decide I should prob plug into power because if battery dies mid update I may turn it
into a coaster instead
* let it do what I should have done in the first place and install everything
* `sudo dnf upgrade` again just in case
* reboot
* success!!!!!!
* I AM A GOD OF COMPUTERS FEAR MY POWER
* Now the keyboard is icky, so cleaned that
* Machine good now! Work good!

Now that the machine is up and running with Fedora, here's what its base resource usage looks
like:

[8 GB of RAM, what is this, a machine for ANTS?]($image.asset('base-system-resources.png'))

My goal with this thing is that I want to replace (almost) everything you can see in this
screenshot with software I've written myself:

[Look at all this stable, amazing OSS software I'm going to rip out and replace with my own OSS junk!]($image.asset('to-replace.png'))

To list it out explicitly though, that's (in rough order of when I plan to tackle them):
1. Application launcher/file finder (a-la spotlight search on Mac, currently I use
   [rofi](https://github.com/davatorium/rofi) on my desktop and KDE's built in KRunner on the
   laptop)
2. Window manager ("compositor" in wayland parlance, which is the protocol I'm going to
   build on). I plan to build against [river](https://codeberg.org/river/river) because it looks neat and I'm currently
   planning to write most/all of this stuff in `zig` anyway, so it'll be useful reference.
3. Status Bar (for time/date, media controls, notifications, etc)
4. Text editor. Shown in the screenshot is my heavily tailored `nvim` config.
5. Terminal emulator. I'm using [ghostty](https://ghostty.org/) on this laptop as a chance to experiment with
   something other than my beloved [wezterm](https://wezterm.org/)
6. Shell + Shell Prompt. You can pry [fish](https://fishshell.com/) from my cold, dead hands, and [starship](https://starship.rs/)
   provides no value other than looking cool, but it will be fun to tinker with something of
   my own anyway.

The terminal emulator and shell I care the least about, I do want to tinker with making
something myself but for those 2 especially I don't intend to replace the tools I normally
use at all. The first 3 on that list are the main ones I care about: I'd consider it a
success if they were good enough pieces of software that I could run them on my desktop as
well without having to constantly squash bugs.

I don't have any time goal in mind for any of this to be clear, it's just a fun little
side project to work on with a physically dedicated space to do so. I'll add another
update post when I have the app launcher to a state that's presentable!
